course: religion-ethics
lesson: 63
title: Burial, cremation, the wake and the grave
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: After a person dies, families care for the body in one of two main ways: burial, where the coffin is lowered into a grave, or cremation, where the body is turned to soft grey ashes by great heat at a crematorium; the person cannot feel anything, because their body has already stopped working.
explain: Picture the cemetery after Grandad's funeral: the coffin goes gently down on straps, the vicar says «earth to earth, ashes to ashes, dust to dust, in sure and certain hope of the resurrection», and Ellie throws a little handful of soil as her goodbye.
explain: Afterwards there is often a wake, sandwiches and cake at a pub or a hall, where grown-ups laugh, cry and share stories; it is not a party because he died, but a way of being together, like the Irish wake, Jewish shiva and the Ukrainian pomynky meal.
explain: A grave or a place where ashes rest gives people somewhere to go and remember: Ellie brings flowers a few weeks later, and Oliver reads «Rest in Peace» on a headstone from 1850, an old prayer that the person may be at peace.
explain: Faiths differ: many Christians choose either burial or cremation, Catholic and Orthodox Christians often prefer burial, Hindus and Sikhs usually cremate, and Muslims and Orthodox Jews bury, Muslims usually in a simple white cloth within a day, as a sign that everyone is equal in death.
fact: In a burial the coffin is lowered into a grave in a cemetery or churchyard.
fact: At a Church of England burial the vicar says «earth to earth, ashes to ashes, dust to dust, in sure and certain hope of the resurrection».
fact: Throwing a little soil on the coffin is an old way of taking part in the burial and saying goodbye; in Jewish funerals mourners often shovel earth into the grave themselves.
fact: Cremation means the body is turned to ashes by great heat at a crematorium, and the ashes are given to the family; the person cannot feel anything.
fact: At many crematorium services curtains close around the coffin at the end, as a sign of goodbye.
fact: A wake is a gathering after a funeral where people eat, talk and share stories; it helps people grieve together.
fact: Ashes look like fine grey sand; families may keep them, bury them or scatter them in a special place.
fact: Catholics are asked to keep ashes in a sacred place, such as a churchyard, rather than at home or scattered; Hindus often scatter ashes in a river such as the Ganges.
fact: «Rest in Peace», RIP, comes from the Latin «Requiescat in pace», an old Christian prayer.
fact: Using «RIP» as a joke can hurt someone who is grieving.
fact: Muslims usually bury their dead within a day, wrapped in a simple white cloth called a shroud; Jews often use a plain wooden coffin.
fact: Visiting a grave and bringing flowers is a way for the living to show love and remember.
exercise: The coffin is lowered into the ground. Ellie worries, «He'll be cold.» What is true? | His body can't feel cold or anything any more | He will be cold until spring | The coffin has a soft blanket inside to keep him warm all winter | He will feel the cold only at night, when the ground freezes
exercise: People throw soil on the coffin. Is that rude? | No, it is an old way of saying goodbye and taking part | Yes, it is meant as an insult to the person who has died and his family | Yes, it shows people are angry with him for leaving | No, it is done to keep the coffin warm under the ground
exercise: Callum's gran is going to be cremated. What does cremated mean? | Her body is turned to ashes by great heat, and she feels nothing | She is frozen in ice until she wakes up | She is buried at sea in a boat | She is kept in a glass case at the church
exercise: At the crematorium, curtains close around the coffin. What does that show? | The moment of saying goodbye | That the service has gone wrong and must stop | That the coffin is being hidden from thieves | That the person inside is changing into new clothes
exercise: After the funeral, grown-ups laugh at the wake. Why a «party»? | It is a way of being together, eating and sharing stories | They are secretly happy that Grandad has finally died | It is a birthday party that was planned long before he died | Funerals in Britain always end with a disco and a cake
exercise: Ellie asks, «Can Grandad see the flowers?» What is the honest answer? | His body can't; flowers show love, and beliefs about the dead differ | Yes, he can see everything from the grave, so the flowers must be fresh | No, because he never liked flowers when he was alive | Yes, but only on Sundays, when the cemetery is open
exercise: Oliver sees «Rest in Peace» on a grave from 1850. Where does RIP come from? | The Latin prayer «Requiescat in pace» | A modern text message abbreviation from the 1990s | An old English swear word used by sailors | The name of a famous Victorian gravedigger
exercise: Kayden's cousin says «RIP» when someone loses a game. Is that bad? | It can hurt someone who has just lost someone | It's always fine, because words never really matter | It's against the law in Britain to say it about games | It makes the loser die in real life
exercise: Mia's family keeps Grandma's ashes in a box at home. Is Gran trapped in there? | No, the ashes are what remains of her body; Gran herself has died | Yes, her spirit lives in the box | Yes, and she can hear the family talk | No, the box is empty
exercise: Freddie's family scatter Grandad's ashes on his favourite hill, and the wind blows some back. Is laughing okay? | Yes, sad moments can have funny bits | No, it ruins the whole goodbye | No, it brings bad luck to the family for ever and ever | Yes, because the ashes are not really Grandad's
exercise: Sophie asks why some people are buried in a shroud, not a coffin. Why? | Muslims usually use a simple white cloth, showing everyone is equal in death | Shrouds are only used for people who were too poor to pay for a proper wooden coffin | Coffins were too expensive that day, so the family had to make do | The white cloth keeps the body warm and dry in the ground
exercise: How quickly do Muslims usually bury their dead? | Within a day if possible | After forty days of prayer | After one whole year has passed | Only on Fridays, the holy day
exercise: Which faiths usually cremate their dead? | Hindus and Sikhs | Muslims and Orthodox Jews | Only Catholics and Anglicans | No faith ever cremates its dead
exercise: What are Catholics asked to do with ashes? | Keep them in a sacred place like a churchyard | Scatter them anywhere they like, even in the garden | Keep them on a shelf at home, close to the family | Throw them into the sea at once, straight after the service
exercise: What do the words «earth to earth, ashes to ashes, dust to dust» remind people? | Bodies return to the earth, with hope of resurrection | Graves must always be filled with sand, not soil | Churches must be swept clean after every funeral service | People turn into ash if they were bad in their lives
test: Callum, scared, asks, «Wots cremated mean? Does it hurt her?» What is the gentle truth? | Her body is turned to ashes, and it doesn't hurt, because she has already died | It hurts, so she should be buried instead | It's a kind of operation to make her better | It means she is sent to the seaside
test: The vicar intones at the grave: «We therefore commit his body to the ground, earth to earth, ashes to ashes, dust to dust, in sure and certain hope of the resurrection.» What is happening? | Grandad's body is being buried, with Christian hope | Grandad is being sent to prison for something he did | The grave is being cleaned with dust before the service begins | The service is being cancelled because of the weather
test: A Gen Z cousin at the wake says, «Why's everyone vibing? Grandad's dead, this is so cringe.» («Vibing» means «relaxing, having a good time».) Best answer? | People are being together and sharing stories; it helps grief | They are glad he died and are celebrating | It's rude, and the wake should stop straight away so people can cry | It's a party for winning the lottery
test: An Irish auntie says, «Sure the wake was great craic, God rest him.» («Craic» means «fun, good company».) Does that mean she isn't sad? | No, people can share good stories and still grieve | Yes, she is glad he is gone and can't hide it any more | Yes, wakes are only for fun, so nobody there is really sad | No, she isn't sad only because she never really knew him
test: What would happen if nobody ever visited graves or remembered the dead? | People would lose a place and a way to feel close to those they loved | The dead would come back to visit us | Graves would turn into gardens by themselves | Nothing at all, remembering never helps
test: Kayden's cousin types «RIP lol» when his mate loses at FIFA. A girl whose nan just died sees it. What is wise? | Think about who's listening; it can hurt someone grieving | It's always funny, so keep doing it whoever is around | Say it louder so everyone in the room laughs along with you | Report the girl for being upset
test: A Gen Alpha girl says, «Gran in a box on the shelf? That's so creepy, skibidi.» («Skibidi» means nothing, just a sound.) What is true? | The ashes are what is left of Gran's body; keeping them is a way to remember | Gran is trapped inside and can hear us | The box is magic and creepy | Ashes turn into ghosts at night
test: Freddie's mate from Sydney says, «Mate, the wind blew Grandad's ashes on ya, too right that's funny!» («Too right» means «definitely».) Is it okay to laugh? | Yes, funny moments happen even at sad times | No, laughing ruins the goodbye and upsets Grandad | No, it is bad luck for ever | Yes, because the ashes were fake and not really Grandad
test: Oliver's big sister, a Gen X mum, says, «Back in my day we read old headstones on school trips, rad history.» («Rad» means «great, cool».) Why might old graves be interesting? | They tell real stories of people who lived long ago | They hide treasure under every stone for children to find | They are only for ghosts to read at night | They were all made last year to look old for tourists
test: A cartoon shows a skeleton climbing out of a grave at night. What is the truth? | The dead stay at rest; such scenes are just made-up stories | It happens in every cemetery at midnight, but nobody sees it | Skeletons climb out once a year | Only very old graves do that, never new ones
test: Sophie's Muslim neighbour is buried the next day in a white cloth. Sophie asks why so simple. What is true? | Muslims believe everyone is equal in death, rich or poor | The family couldn't afford a proper wooden coffin for him | White cloth is cheaper than wood | It was a mistake made by the funeral director that morning
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the process of cremation? | The reduction of the body to ashes by intense heat, after death, without sensation | A surgical procedure performed upon the living | The preservation of the body in ice | The interment of the body at sea
exam: Identify the principal purpose of a wake held subsequent to a funeral. | The gathering of the bereaved for mutual comfort, refreshment and remembrance | The celebration of the death of the deceased | The division of the estate of the deceased | The appointment of a successor to the deceased
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the abbreviation «RIP». | It derives from the Latin prayer «Requiescat in pace» | It derives from a modern electronic messaging convention | It derives from an expression of the gravediggers' guild | It derives from an Old English oath
exam: Ascertain the customary period within which Muslims inter the deceased. | Within one day, where practicable | Within forty days of the death | Within one calendar year of the death | At a date chosen by the eldest son
